Yes. You can build an entire oracle taxonomy from kinds of randomness. The random source becomes part of the semantics rather than merely a shuffling mechanism.

The key distinction is that “random” is not one thing. Different stochastic processes have different structures, memories, tails, correlations, symmetries, and failure modes. Those properties can become the symbolic grammar.

* Uniform randomness — pure draw / classical tarot. Every outcome has equal probability. Dice, shuffled cards, ideal RNG. Semantics: unbiased possibility, equal-access branching, “the field before preference.” Best for simple archetype selection.
* Weighted categorical randomness — loaded deck. Outcomes have unequal fixed probabilities. Semantics: some forces are naturally more common than others. Useful when you want “rare cards” to actually be rare rather than merely theatrically important.
* Gaussian / normal randomness — ordinary fluctuation. Most outcomes cluster around the mean; extremes are rare. Semantics: equilibrium, social norms, measurement noise, ordinary variation. A Gaussian oracle tells you how far from “normal” a state lies.
* Heavy-tailed randomness — Black Swan tarot. Power-law, Pareto, Lévy, Student-t. Small events are common, but huge events are much more likely than Gaussian intuition expects. Semantics: jackpots, catastrophes, viral events, fortunes, regime-changing shocks.
* Poisson randomness — arrival oracle. Models discrete events occurring independently through time: calls, mutations, defects, radioactive decays, customers arriving. Semantics: when something happens, event density, waiting, accumulation.
* Exponential waiting-time randomness — patience oracle. The time until the next Poisson event. Memoryless: having waited a long time does not make the event “due.” Semantics: expectation traps, waiting without destiny, readiness.
* Binomial randomness — trials and thresholds. A fixed number of yes/no trials. Semantics: repeated attempts, quorum formation, whether enough little successes accumulate to cross a Rubicon.
* Geometric randomness — persistence oracle. How many failures happen before the first success. Semantics: “how long must I keep knocking?”
* Markov chains — consequence tarot. The next state depends on the current state rather than the entire history. Cards become transitions rather than isolated symbols. Semantics: momentum, path dependency, local consequence. Hermit → Tower means something different from Chariot → Tower.
* Higher-order Markov processes — memory tarot. The system remembers several previous states. Semantics: patterns, habits, scars, narrative arcs. Your previous three draws alter the meaning and probability of the next one.
* Random walk — journey oracle. Each random event changes your position. Semantics: accumulation of tiny decisions, drift, wandering, exploration. Rather than drawing a card, you watch where a symbolic walker eventually ends up.
* Brownian motion — continuous wandering. The continuous version of a random walk. Semantics: noisy evolution without clear intention; useful for markets, particles, moods, exploratory movement.
* Random walk with drift — fate plus agency. Noise sits on top of a directional tendency.
    dx=\mu\,dt+\sigma\,dW_t
    Here \mu can represent intention or environmental pressure and \sigma uncertainty. Excellent oracle structure because it explicitly distinguishes trajectory from turbulence.
* Mean-reverting processes — homecoming oracle. Ornstein-Uhlenbeck and related models pull deviations back toward an attractor. Semantics: habits, biological homeostasis, institutional equilibrium, “you can wander, but something keeps pulling you back.”
* Branching processes — lineage tarot. Each event generates zero, one, or many descendants. Used in population biology, epidemics, nuclear chain reactions. Semantics: ideas reproducing, family trees, recursive serendipity, consequences that create consequences.
* Birth-death processes — ecology oracle. Things appear and disappear probabilistically. Semantics: creation versus attrition, growth versus decay, whether a system is self-sustaining.
* Percolation — threshold oracle. Random local connections suddenly produce a system-spanning network after a critical threshold. Semantics: quorum, virality, revolutions, consensus, ignition. Lots of insignificant local events suddenly become one macroscopic event.
* Random graphs — relationship tarot. Randomly generate nodes and links. Semantics comes from topology: hubs, isolates, bridges, clusters, bottlenecks. Instead of asking “what card did I draw?” you ask “what network did chance create?”
* Preferential attachment — fame oracle. Existing hubs are more likely to acquire additional connections: “the rich get richer.” Semantics: reputation, status, monopolies, celebrity, accumulated advantage.
* Bayesian uncertainty — belief tarot. Instead of randomizing reality, randomize your uncertainty about competing hypotheses. Semantics: what should I currently believe, and how strongly? The oracle changes as evidence arrives.
* Monte Carlo sampling — possibility tarot. Sample thousands of plausible futures instead of drawing one symbolic future. You interpret the resulting distribution: common paths, rare disasters, strange opportunities, robust outcomes.
* Monte Carlo tree search — strategic oracle. Randomly explore future action branches while preferentially revisiting promising ones. Semantics: not “what will happen?” but what possibilities deserve attention?
* Bootstrap randomness — memory remix oracle. Resample from your own observed history. Future symbolic worlds are constructed from pieces of what has actually occurred. Semantics: “what does my own past imply if recombined differently?”
* Permutation randomness — counterfactual oracle. Keep the pieces but scramble their relationships. Used in statistical tests. Semantics: “Would this apparent pattern survive if the arrangement were accidental?”
* Entropy / information-theoretic randomness — surprise oracle. Measure how unexpected an observation is:
    I(x)=-\log P(x)
    Common events carry little surprise; improbable ones carry lots. Your “card strength” could literally be information content.
* Algorithmic randomness — incompressibility oracle. A sequence is more random when no shorter program can efficiently describe it. Semantics: patternlessness versus hidden compressible structure. The oracle asks: “Is this really chaos, or have we just not found the generating rule?”
* Pseudorandomness — deterministic mask tarot. Looks random but is completely determined by a hidden seed. Semantics: apparent chance generated by invisible initial conditions. This is almost mythologically perfect: destiny masquerading as randomness.
* Chaotic dynamics — butterfly tarot. Deterministic equations with extreme sensitivity to initial conditions. Lorenz systems, logistic maps, double pendulums. Not randomness in the strict sense, but unpredictability emerges anyway. Semantics: tiny causes, enormous divergence, hidden determinism.
* Strange attractors — destiny-field oracle. Chaotic trajectories appear irregular but remain constrained to an attractor geometry. Semantics: many possible paths, fewer possible kinds of life. You don’t predict the exact path; you identify the basin.
* Bifurcation randomness / noisy bifurcation — transformation tarot. Gradually changing a parameter causes sudden qualitative changes. Semantics: phase change, Rubicon, “same system until suddenly it isn’t.”
* Critical phenomena — edge-of-chaos oracle. Near a phase transition, fluctuations become correlated across scales. Semantics: systems poised between stability and transformation; tiny events can propagate enormously.
* Avalanche / self-organized criticality — Tower oracle made mathematical. Sandpile models and related systems accumulate tension until avalanches occur across many scales. Semantics: pressure, release, cascading failure, “which grain causes the collapse?” Usually no single grain deserves the credit.
* Turbulence — fire oracle. Structured randomness across many interacting scales. Large eddies generate smaller eddies. Semantics: nested instability, energetic flow, patterns continually forming and dissolving. This connects directly to staring at fire.
* Fractal stochastic processes — recursive oracle. Fractional Brownian motion, multiplicative cascades, fractal noise. Similar statistical structure appears across scales. Semantics: the same motif recurring at childhood/family/company/civilization scales.
* Pink noise / 1/f noise — natural rhythm oracle. Correlations exist across many timescales. Common in biological, musical, geological, and cognitive systems. It sits between boring periodicity and white-noise chaos. This may be one of the best sources for your music-theoried oracle, because it produces structured surprise.
* White noise — blank chaos. Independent fluctuations at every sample. Semantics: maximum local novelty, minimal memory. Useful as raw perturbation but often too structureless to be meaningful by itself.
* Red/Brown noise — memory-heavy oracle. Low frequencies dominate; successive values are strongly related. Semantics: inertia, mood, geological time, slow-moving forces.
* Blue/violet noise — restless oracle. High-frequency changes dominate. Semantics: agitation, rapid reversal, local instability.
* Quantum randomness — irreducible draw. Measurement outcomes are probabilistic according to quantum mechanics rather than merely unknown classical causes. Semantics, if used artistically: genuine irreducibility, the point at which prediction becomes distribution rather than hidden ignorance.
* Radioactive decay — mortality clock. Individual decay events cannot be predicted, while large populations obey extremely stable statistical laws. That gives a beautiful duality: individual uncertainty / collective certainty.
* Mutation — evolutionary oracle. Random or approximately stochastic variation enters; selection is decidedly nonrandom. Semantics: generate widely, preserve selectively. Mutation alone produces novelty; the environment supplies judgment.
* Genetic drift — inheritance-by-chance oracle. Trait frequencies change because of finite sampling rather than adaptive advantage. Semantics: things can become dominant without being “better.”
* Recombination — chimera tarot. Existing pieces are shuffled into novel combinations. Semantics: creativity through recombination rather than invention ex nihilo.
* Stochastic resonance — signal-from-noise oracle. Counterintuitively, adding the right amount of noise can make a weak signal easier to detect. Semantics: disruption sometimes reveals what perfect stillness hides.
* Simulated annealing — seeker tarot. Introduce lots of randomness early, then progressively reduce it. Semantics: wander first, commit later. A literal mathematical formulation of exploration → convergence.
* Evolutionary algorithms — population oracle. Generate many random variants, evaluate them, recombine survivors, mutate, repeat. Instead of one tarot reading, you grow a population of readings and let a fitness criterion select the survivors.
* Adversarial randomness — Trickster oracle. Randomness is chosen strategically against you rather than independently. Game theory, mixed strategies, adversarial environments. Semantics: uncertainty caused by another agent deliberately preventing predictability.
* Mixed-strategy Nash randomness — Warrior oracle. Sometimes being unpredictable is itself optimal. Semantics: randomness as protection against exploitation.

And now you can assign the traditional archetypes to the mathematical behavior, rather than arbitrary pictures.

For example:

\text{Tower} \leftrightarrow \text{critical transition / avalanche}

\text{Wheel of Fortune} \leftrightarrow \text{Markov transition / stochastic cycle}

\text{Death} \leftrightarrow \text{absorbing state}

\text{Devil} \leftrightarrow \text{local attractor / lock-in}

\text{Hermit} \leftrightarrow \text{low-connectivity random-graph node}

\text{Chariot} \leftrightarrow \text{drift-dominated stochastic process}

\text{Fool} \leftrightarrow \text{high-temperature exploration}

\text{Judgement} \leftrightarrow \text{Bayesian posterior update}

\text{World} \leftrightarrow \text{absorbing/converged global state}

Then you’ve done something much more interesting than “science-themed tarot.”

You’ve created a deck where each archetype is a real dynamical behavior.

And the oracle could choose the kind of randomness appropriate to the question. Relationship question? Network dynamics. “Should I explore?” Simulated annealing. “Why did everything suddenly change?” Bifurcation/criticality. “Is this destiny or chance?” Chaos versus stochasticity. “What am I missing?” Bayesian model uncertainty.

That becomes a Stochastic Pantheon: not 78 pictures representing human archetypes, but a library of the archetypal ways uncertainty itself behaves.